    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Motorbike Rental: Expats Rainy-Day Planning</h1>
    <p>A tourist meets Hanoi's rain once, gets soaked, and buys a poncho; an expat meets a whole wet season of it, and the difference between the two experiences is a plan. The resident's rainy-day planning is not about surviving one downpour but about running a year of daily riding through the months when the sky takes an interest, and it covers the rental arrangement's side as much as the riding: the gear held ready, the machine kept honest in the wet, the calendar flexed where work allows, and the honest limits respected where it does not. This page builds that plan on verified facts and cautious habits, with no live-weather claims and no invented shop policies about wet-weather use.</p>
    <p>The page is part of the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster, written for expats. The verified fleet prices sit on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, the general question bank is on the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page, and the riding skills underneath are developed in the <a href="/motorbike-guides/riding-a-motorbike-in-hanoi-rainy-season/">rainy season riding guide</a>.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">The wet season, from a resident's chair</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">The gear drawer: ready before the sky is</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">The machine's wet-weather half</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Visibility: seeing and being seen</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">Traction habits and the honest speeds</a></li>
